  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Zapotec\"\nsubtitle: \"Mexico\"\nregion: \"Latin America\"\ntags: [ethnography, latin-america]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | Indigenous people of Mexico |\n| Where | Southern state of Oaxaca, with communities in Puebla, Veracruz, Guerrero, Mexico City, Los Angeles, California's Central Valley and New Jersey |\n| How many | 400,000 to 650,000; about 500,000 speakers of a Zapotec language |\n| Language | Zapotec, a tonal language family of around fifty to sixty varieties in the Oto-Manguean stock |\n| Religion | Largely Catholic, with older practices visible inside it |\n| Known for | Monte Albán, one of Mesoamerica's first writing systems · Burnished black pottery · Backstrap-loom textiles with warp patterning · Danza de la Pluma dance · Tonal languages used for word play |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Zapotec are an Indigenous people concentrated in Oaxaca, Mexico, with a population of 400,000 to 650,000 speakers across multiple regional varieties. Their pre-Columbian civilization centred on Monte Albán produced one of Mesoamerica's first writing systems. The Zapotec languages form a main branch of the Oto-Manguean family, with regional divisions including the istmeños, serranos, southern Zapotec, and Central Valley Zapotec. Today they are largely Catholic, though older religious practices remain visible.\n\n## Material culture\n\nZapotec material culture centres on textiles made on the backstrap loom, burnished black pottery, and carved wooden objects, with Spanish colonial influences absorbed into Indigenous forms.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nBackstrap-loom weaving produces patterned cotton and wool textiles in distinctive geometric and figural motifs.\n\n- **Huipil** — A women's upper garment built from a rectangle with a cut neck opening, made of blue cotton with white flowers or organza with green brocade and imported lace and ribbon.\n- **Falda** — A wrap skirt of two flared panels woven of red cotton with weft-stripe patterning, embroidered with geometric motifs in multiple colours.\n- **Rebozo** — A wool shawl woven on the backstrap loom with warp-patterned multicoloured stripes and finger-knotted warp fringe.\n- **Morral** — A knitted wool shoulder bag in dark brown worked with mirror-image bird-like figures in white and rows of letters along the edges.\n- **Sashes and girdles** — Narrow bands woven on the backstrap loom with warp-patterned cotton or silk, some carrying geometric motifs identified as scorpions or dancers from the Danza de la Pluma.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nWomen combine the huipil or embroidered blusa with a wrapped falda, bound by a woven girdle or sash, and dress the head with a rodete and braided wool ornaments.\n\n- **Rodete** — A turban of black wool hair cords joined at one end and left as a long fringe at the other.\n- **Blusa** — A white plain-weave blouse with short gathered sleeves, satin-stitch embroidered in multiple colours with flower motifs and finished with a red yarn tassel.\n- **Juchiteca skirt and huipil** (*Juchiteca*) — On the Isthmus, the recognised native outfit worn by women and by muxes, the third-gender members of Isthmus Zapotec communities.\n\n### Architecture\n\nThe Zapotecs were a sedentary people building stone and mortar houses, with monumental architecture centred on Monte Albán, the largest city in the southern Mexican highlands until about AD 700.\n\n- **Monte Albán** — Founded on a mountain overlooking the three Central Valleys, with monumental buildings, ball courts, tombs and the arrowhead-shaped Building J, the Conquest Slab, faced with carved stones bearing hieroglyphic place-glyphs and ruler heads.\n- **Yo hopèe** — The house of the vital force where priests performed rites.\n- **Mitla** — Seat of the paramount priest, known for its ruins.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nBurnished black pottery is the signature ware, with vessels often pierced or etched with floral and figural designs, alongside modelled figurines, whistles and household equipment.\n\n- **Burnished black pottery** — The signature ware including pots with cut-out flowers and leaves, modelled figurines and toys in the form of parrot, bird, lizard, lion, fish and turtle, and whistles shaped as peacock, rooster and duck.\n- **Olla** — A pot with orange slip, black firing marks across the lower half and a burnished sheen.\n- **Cesto de palma** — Palm baskets plaited in two-over-two twill over a square foundation with a round bottom and a rolled rim.\n- **Cerbatana** — A blowgun used for hunting wild game, still made in Oaxaca de Juárez in the 1940s.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nAncient Zapotec artisans worked gold and jade for elite jewelry; present-day adornment uses ceramic birds and fish alternating on necklaces and fibre hair ornaments.\n\n- **Jade earspools and beads** (*Jade*) — Worked by elite artists and valued for its scarcity, included in dedication caches at temples.\n- **Black pottery necklace** — Strung from seven burnished black pottery birds alternating with black pottery fish.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nZapotec music appears primarily through performance context, with musicians playing in white clothing and sombreros alongside elaborate feathered-headdress dancers.\n\n- **Whistling** — Used by Zapotec people to communicate in a way their colonizers would not detect, sitting alongside the tonal structure of the languages.\n- **Toy flute** — A burnished black clay flute with four finger holes and a snake carved between them.\n- **Pottery whistles** — Shaped as peacock, rooster and duck with the mouthpiece in the tail.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nThe Danza de la Pluma is the one named dance, performed by dancers in elaborate painted-feather headdresses, and serves a social function in the separation of the sexes.\n\n- **Danza de la Pluma** — A dance important enough to become a textile motif, performed by dancers in elaborate headdresses of painted feathers facing musicians and spectators in an open space.\n- **Velas** — The reference point for public assembly on the Isthmus, where men and women work separately and remain apart during ritual occasions except when dancing.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nMajor religious observances include the pilgrimage to the Virgen de Juquila and the Day of the Dead, with pre-Hispanic ritual including dedication of buildings by cache and evidence of human sacrifice.\n\n- **Virgen de Juquila** — A Marian devotion founded at Santa Catarina Juquila in Oaxaca, drawing many Zapotec Catholics on an annual pilgrimage during festivities running from 7 to 9 December.\n- **Day of the Dead** — A festival for which carved wooden horses and riders were made for sale, and skull-headed female figurines were carved.\n- **Copa pitào** — The priests, drawn mostly from the nobility, with commoners admitted only to the lower ranks, required to train in religious practice before taking power.\n- **Cuilapan Temple Pyramid cache** (*Cuilapan*) — A dedication cache dated to AD 700 holding jade beads, jade earspools, obsidian blades, shells, stones, a pearl and small bird bones, dedicating the structure to power, sacrifice and the link between underworld and cosmos.\n\n## Foodways\n\nMaize is central to Zapotec agriculture and religion, with the Central Valleys considered one of the cradles of maize, and irrigation extending cultivation beyond the rivers.\n\n- **Pitao Cozobi** — The primary god of the Valley Zapotecs, god of maize and agriculture.\n- **Coqui Lao** — God of turkey hens.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nOrigin legends describe the Zapotec as the original people of the valley of Oaxaca, born from rocks or descended from big cats, with a logosyllabic script attested from about 500 BC.\n\n- **Danzante stone** — Monument 3 from San José Mogote bearing a logosyllabic script, one of Mesoamerica's first, attested from about 500 BC.\n- **Be'ena' Za'a** — The Cloud People, the self-designation based on the belief that the governing elite descended from supernatural beings living among the clouds.\n- **Bën za** — The Valley Zapotec term meaning People of the clouds.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nZapotec comprises around fifty to sixty mutually unintelligible varieties with time depth comparable to the Romance languages, all tonal and contrasting phonation types in vowels.\n\n- **Diidxazá** — The name for the language in Juchitán, with the first element meaning word.\n- **Didxsaj** — The name for the language in Mitla, with the first element meaning word.\n- **Cocijo** — The rain god, whom San Pedro resembles in Catholic practice.\n- **Pè** — The life force residing in wind and breath in the pre-conquest polytheistic religion.\n- **Ouija-tào** — The great seer, who topped the hierarchy of deities and resided at Mitla.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Huipil* — Women's upper garment built from a rectangle with a cut neck opening\n- *Falda* — A wrap skirt of two flared panels with ties\n- *Rodete* — A turban of black wool hair cords worn on the head\n- *Rebozo* — A wool shawl woven on the backstrap loom\n- *Morral* — A knitted wool shoulder bag\n- *Cesto de palma* — Palm baskets plaited in two-over-two twill\n- *Cerbatana* — A blowgun used for hunting wild game\n- *Danza de la Pluma* — A dance performed with elaborate painted-feather headdresses\n- *Velas* — Reference point for public assembly on the Isthmus\n- *Virgen de Juquila* — A Marian devotion founded at Santa Catarina Juquila in Oaxaca\n- *Copa pitào* — Priests, drawn mostly from the nobility\n- *Pitao Cozobi* — The primary god of the Valley Zapotecs, god of maize and agriculture\n- *Coqui Lao* — God of turkey hens\n- *Bën za* — Valley Zapotec term meaning People of the clouds\n- *Be'ena' Za'a* — The Cloud People, based on belief that the elite descended from supernatural beings living among the clouds\n- *Pè* — The life force residing in wind and breath\n- *Cocijo* — The rain god in pre-conquest religion\n- *Ouija-tào* — The great seer, who topped the hierarchy of deities and resided at Mitla\n- *Diidxazá* — The name for the language in Juchitán\n- *Istmeños* — Zapotec people of the Isthmus of Tehuantepec\n- *Serranos* — Zapotec people of the Sierra Madre de Oaxaca\n- *Muxes* — Third-gender members of Isthmus Zapotec communities\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Michael Lind, *Ancient Zapotec Religion*, University Press of Colorado, 2015\n- Joseph W. Whitecotton, *The Zapotecs: Princes, Priests, and Peasants*, University of Oklahoma Press, 1984\n- Anya Peterson Royce, *Becoming an Ancestor: The Isthmus Zapotec Way of Death*, SUNY Press, Albany, 2011\n- Beverly Chinas, *The Isthmus Zapotecs: Women's Roles in Cultural Context*, Rinehart and Winston, New York, 1973\n- Joyce Marcus and Kent V. Flannery on the formation and expansion of the Monte Albán state; Alfonso Caso's 1930s excavations at Monte Albán; S. Linné, \"Modern Blowguns in Oaxaca, Mexico\", *Ethnos* 1948:3–4\n- The Archive of the Indigenous Languages of Latin America: Southern Zapotec collection of Rosemary Beam de Azcona, Zapotec collection of Brook Danielle Lillehaugen, Isthmus Zapotec collection of Gabriela Pérez Báez\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zapotec_peoples\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zapotec_language\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zapotec_religion\n- https://folkways.si.edu/search?query=Zapotec\n- British Museum collection online (textiles, black pottery and Oaxaca dance photographs shown here): https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection\n- Världskulturmuseerna — Museum of World Culture and Museum of Ethnography, Stockholm (Oaxaca blusa, falda, morral, palm baskets and Bodil Christensen's 1951 photographs): https://www.varldskulturmuseerna.se/\n\n",
